re control and predictability to users\n- It allows for graceful degradation of context rather than sudden failures\n\n## Your Point is Valid\n\nYou're absolutely correct that the **backend context limit is the hard limit**. In practice, if you're using `COMPRESSION_ENABLED=1` (which you could enable for testing), the system would still respect:\n\n1. Your backend's actual context limit\n2. The `SAFETY_MARGIN` parameter (which reserves space for responses)\n3. The effective context limit calculation\n\nThe message-based trigger is essentially a **safety net** that provides earlier intervention before the conversation gets too large.\n\n## Recommendation for Your Testing\n\nFor your token limit testing purposes, you might consider:\n1. **Setting both thresholds high** to test your actual backend limits\n2. **Using the token-based approach only** if you want cleaner, more predictable behavior\n3. **Monitoring the logs** to see when compression is triggered\n\nBut your intuition is right - in the end, the backend context limit is indeed the ultimate constraint, and a token-based approach would be more direct. The current dual-trigger system exists more for flexibility and user experience than for technical necessity."
