hey represent transparency about what still needs investigation\n    35|- **Transparency is valuable:** Documenting unknowns ([PENDING], ASSUMPTIONS) is good practice, not a failure\n    36|\n    37|---\n    38|\n    39|## A1 - WORKFLOW\n    40|\n    41|### A1.1 INVESTIGATE + DESIGN\n    42|\n    43|**UNIFIED INVESTIGATION + DESIGN APPROACH:**\n    44|\n    45|Combined natural discovery + systematic checklist verification with design decisions made in the same cycle as findings:\n    46|\n    47|**INVESTIGATION WORKFLOW:**\n    48|\n    49|FOR each investigation cycle:\n    50|\n    51|1. **Read relevant files naturally** (data structures, contracts, entry points, core business logic components)\n    52|   - While reading, check relevant C1/C2/C3/P1 checklist items\n    53|   - Notice patterns, anomalies, violations while reading\n    54|\n    55|2. **When pattern noticed:**\n    56|   - **DISCOVERY:** Search systematically for ALL instances (grep/codebase_search)\n    57|     - Find WHERE patterns exist\n    58|     - Output: \"Searched for X, found N instances in components: component_a, component_b, component_c\"\n    59|     - Code snippets in search results are NOT evidence - only tell WHERE to look\n    60|   - **VERIFICATION:** See V1 - Verification Standard\n    61|     - Output: \"Read code at component_a:lines; component_b:lines; component_c:lines\"\n    62|     - MUST call read_file even if snippets seen in codebase_search\n    63|   - **CHECK ALL RELATED CATEGORIES:** When a pattern is noticed, check ALL relevant checklist categories:\n    64|     - Example: If infrastructure access pattern noticed \u2192 Check C1.5 (infrastructure abstraction) AND C1.6 (code duplication) AND C2.2 (infrastructure access consistency) AND C2.4 (consistency boundaries)\n    65|     - Example: If error handling pattern noticed \u2192 Check C2.1 (error handling consistency) AND C1.6 (code duplication if error handling duplicated)\n    66|     - **CRITICAL:** One pattern may violate multiple categories - document each violation separately\n    67|\n    68|3. **Document findings immediately:**\n    69|   - Add to tracker with proper status ([PENDING], [PARTIALLY VERIFIED], [VERIFIED])\n    70|   - **INTERNALLY (for AI verification):** Collect detailed evidence: component names, instances, discovery paths, verification confirmation\n    71|   - **OUTPUT (for human):** Show only summary line + concise pattern explanation (detailed evidence not displayed to avoid cognitive overload)\n    72|   - **COUNT COMPONENTS:** List component names from read_file calls internally, count distinct components (NOT instances/offsets)\n    73|   - **STATUS:** See S1 for status meanings. Apply V1 verification standard to determine status.\n    74|   - If architectural violation verified per V1: Add to ARCHITECTURAL ISSUES\n    75|   - **CRITICAL:** Document ALL violations found across ALL categories (C1, C2, C3, P1), not just the \"primary\" or \"biggest\" issue\n    76|   - **CRITICAL:** Each violation is separate - document C1.4, C1.5, C1.6, C2.1, C2.2, etc. as distinct findings even if they seem related\n    77|   - **CRITICAL:** Do not skip documenting violations because they seem \"secondary\" or will be \"fixed by larger changes\" - all violations must be tracked\n    78|   - **CRITICAL:** AI must still complete all verification steps per V1 (document discovery paths internally) even though detailed evidence is not shown in output\n    79|\n    80|4. **Make design decisions in the same cycle as findings:**\n    81|   - When architectural violations are discovered, propose design solutions in the same investigation cycle\n    82|   - When patterns are discovered, decide how to reuse or follow them in the same cycle\n    83|   - Do not defer design decisions to later cycles\n    84|   - Verify design decisions against relevant checklist items\n    85|   - Document design decisions in tracker DESIGN section as they are made\n    86|   - **Plan implementation strategy when context is fresh:** When design decisions are made, also plan implementation strategy (affected components from dependency graph, approach, verification steps). Strategy guides implementation execution during A1.2.\n    87|   - **EVALUATE ARCHITECTURAL ALTERNATIVES:** BEFORE planning implementation strategy, evaluate where logic should live:\n    88|     - [ ] Checked for existing components that handle similar concerns?\n    89|       - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Search for existing components that handle similar concerns.\n    90|       - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Searched for similar components, found: component_names OR none exist]\n    91|     - [ ] Evaluated architectural alternatives (where should this logic live)?\n    92|       - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Consider multiple options: new component, existing component, modification to existing component.\n    93|       - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Evaluated alternatives: option1 (pros/cons), option2 (pros/cons), chosen: option with reasoning]\n    94|     - [ ] Considered separation of concerns (does this belong in chosen location)?\n    95|       - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Evaluate if chosen location violates separation of concerns or architectural boundaries.\n    96|       - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Separation analysis: chosen_location appropriate because reason]\n    97|     - **NOTE:** Architectural evaluation happens during investigation when problem context is fresh. Implementation strategy must include chosen location with justification.\n    98|   - Example: Finding \"Missing abstraction layer\" \u2192 Propose \"Abstraction pattern with interfaces X, Y, Z\" in DESIGN section \u2192 Evaluate alternatives (new component vs existing) \u2192 Plan implementation strategy: affected components, approach, verification steps\n    99|\n   100|5. **Verify comprehensive checklist coverage:**\n   101|   - Before ending investigation cycle, systematically verify all relevant C1/C2/C3/P1 categories were checked\n   102|   - For each category relevant to investigation scope:\n   103|     - [ ] Checked for violations in this category?\n   104|     - [ ] If violations found, documented in tracker with proper status?\n   105|     - [ ] If no violations found, documented as [VERIFIED] with evidence OR marked as not applicable?\n   106|   - **CRITICAL:** ALL violations must be documented regardless of priority or whether they'll be fixed together\n   107|   - **CRITICAL:** Do not prioritize one category over others - check all systematically\n   108|   - **CRITICAL:** Tracker must show comprehensive view of ALL issues across ALL categories, not just prioritized subset\n   109|\n   110|**CRITICAL RULE:** See V1 - Verification Standard. grep/codebase_search are discovery tools, read_file is verification tool. Must complete V1 verification before marking [VERIFIED] per S1.\n   111|\n   112|**CRITICAL: Documentation of Discovery Path**\n   113|- ALWAYS document HOW discovered internally (for AI verification) - not shown in output\n   114|- Natural: \"Noticed while reading component:line_range\" OR targeted: \"Searched for pattern X\"\n   115|- Reference tool calls internally: \"Discovered while reading component_a:268-293 (read_file call #3), then searched (codebase_search call #5)\"\n   116|- Evidence must come from read_file tool calls per V1 (not grep/codebase_search snippets)\n   117|- Discovery path collected internally but not displayed in FINDINGS output (only summary shown to human)\n   118|\n   119|**PARALLEL INVESTIGATION (Efficiency):**\n   120|- Group related checklist items together when they share similar search/verification needs\n   121|- Example: C1.1 (component boundaries) + C1.2 (dependency direction) can be checked together\n   122|- Example: C2.1 (error handling) + C2.2 (infrastructure access consistency) can share component reads\n   123|- Example: C1.5 (infrastructure abstraction) + C1.6 (code duplication) + C2.2 (infrastructure access consistency) can be checked together\n   124|- Batch read_file calls for related items when reading same components\n   125|- Update tracker with multiple findings per round\n   126|- **CRITICAL:** When checking one category, also check related categories that may have violations\n   127|- **CRITICAL:** Document findings for ALL categories checked, not just the most obvious violation\n   128|\n   129|**FEATURE-SPECIFIC INVESTIGATION:**\n   130|\n   131|When designing features that involve data operations (filtering, searching, querying, transformation, etc.):\n   132|- **C2.3:** Verify constraint validation patterns (grep validation usage, verify per V1)\n   133|- **C2.3:** Verify range/type validation patterns (grep parameter validation, verify per V1)\n   134|- **P1.2:** Verify infrastructure access efficiency for operations (read infrastructure access components, check optimization)\n   135|- **C1.6:** Verify operation logic not duplicated (grep operation patterns, verify per V1)\n   136|- **C1.4:** Verify data access patterns (verify per V1 for components performing similar operations)\n   137|\n   138|**CRITICAL:** Reading type/constraint definitions alone insufficient. Verify usage/validation patterns per V1.\n   139|\n   140|**Issue handling:**\n   141|\n   142|- [ ] Architectural violation found (C1/C2/C3/P1 pattern violation)?\n   143|  - Found 1 instance via read_file? \u2192 Mark [PARTIALLY VERIFIED] in FINDINGS, propose design solution in DESIGN section, continue searching for more instances\n   144|  - Verified per V1? \u2192 Mark [VERIFIED] per S1 in FINDINGS, add to ARCHITECTURAL ISSUES with verified evidence, propose design solution in DESIGN section, continue investigating\n   145|  - Not verified yet (no read_file evidence)? \u2192 Continue verification (do not add to ARCHITECTURAL ISSUES until verified)\n   146|  - Document ALL violations found, even if they seem related or will be fixed together. Each violation is separate.\n   147|  - For each violation, propose design solution in the same cycle. Do not defer design to later cycles.\n   148|\n   149|- [ ] Multiple violations found from same pattern or related patterns?\n   150|  - YES \u2192 Document EACH violation separately in tracker with its specific category (C1.4, C1.5, C1.6, C2.1, C2.2, C2.4, etc.)\n   151|  - Do not combine into single \"architectural shortcoming\" - each category violation is distinct\n   152|  - Example: Missing data access layer violates C1.5 (data access abstraction) AND code duplication violates C1.6 (code duplication) - these are TWO separate violations, both must be documented\n   153|  - **CRITICAL:** One code pattern may violate multiple categories - check and document all of them\n   154|\n   155|- [ ] All relevant categories checked for violations?\n   156|  - When investigating an area, check ALL relevant C1/C2/C3/P1 categories, not just the most obvious one\n   157|  - Example: When investigating infrastructure access \u2192 Check C1.5, C1.6, C2.2, C2.4, P1.2, P1.3\n   158|  - Document findings for each category checked, even if no violations found\n   159|\n   160|- [ ] Related blocking issues exist (architectural OR design)?\n   161|  - YES \u2192 Make design decision to address blocking issue, then mark [VERIFIED]\n   162|  - NO \u2192 Evidence: [No blocking issues found]\n   163|  \n   164|- [ ] Design solution proposed for violation?\n   165|  - NO \u2192 Propose design solution in DESIGN section, then continue investigation\n   166|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Design decision documented in DESIGN section]\n   167|\n   168|**DESIGN DECISIONS:**\n   169|\n   170|Two levels of design decisions:\n   171|\n   172|**1. Design Direction [DIRECTION]:** High-level approach identified when findings discovered\n   173|- Identified immediately when issue or requirement discovered\n   174|- Does NOT require concrete implementation details\n   175|- Mark as [DIRECTION] in DESIGN section\n   176|\n   177|**2. Concrete Design Decisions:** Specific implementation details\n   178|- Requires investigation of actual patterns in codebase\n   179|- Can be marked [PENDING] if investigation needed to make concrete\n   180|- Mark as [VERIFIED] when concrete design complete\n   181|\n   182|**WORKFLOW:**\n   183|1. Finding or requirement discovered \u2192 Identify design direction [DIRECTION]\n   184|2. Investigate patterns needed for concrete design \u2192 Make concrete design decisions [VERIFIED]\n   185|3. Document both in DESIGN section with appropriate status\n   186|4. Continue investigation with design in mind\n   187|\n   188|**FOR each design decision:**\n   189|- Check tracker findings for existing patterns serving same role\n   190|- If pattern exists \u2192 Reuse it (document in DESIGN section as [VERIFIED] with evidence)\n   191|- If pattern doesn't exist \u2192 Propose new pattern based on architecture discovered (document in DESIGN section)\n   192|- Verify design decision against relevant C1/C2/C3/P1 checklist items\n   193|- **Plan implementation strategy when design decision is made:**\n   194|  - Identify affected components (from dependency graph and systematic search)\n   195|  - Plan approach (create new component, move/rename identifier, refactor pattern, etc.)\n   196|  - Plan verification steps (what to check after implementation)\n   197|  - Document strategy in DESIGN section alongside design decision\n   198|  - Strategy guides implementation execution during A1.2 when context may be less fresh\n   199|- If design direction identified but concrete details need investigation \u2192 Mark as [PENDING] with \"Needs: Investigation of [specific patterns/behaviors/operations]\"\n   200|- If design depends on assumptions that need verification \u2192 Mark as [CONDITIONAL] with \"Depends on: [assumption from ASSUMPTIONS section]\"\n   201|- If design is complete and verified \u2192 Mark as [VERIFIED] with evidence\n   202|- If design has blocking issues discovered during investigation \u2192 Add to DESIGN ISSUES section\n   203|\n   204|**DESIGN OUTPUT FORMAT:**\n   205|- **INTERNALLY (for AI):** Collect detailed design information: implementation approach, structure, methods, patterns, rationale, specific component:line references, implementation strategy, implementation strategy\n   206|- **OUTPUT (for human):** Show concise, readable format optimized for human consumption\n   207|- **Format structure:**\n   208|  - **For related decisions:** Group with a summary line, then list key decisions as sub-items\n   209|  - **For standalone decisions:** Single entry with Decision + Rationale + Reference + Implementation Strategy + Implementation Strategy\n   210|  - **Decision summary:** Clear WHAT (what is being decided), keep brief\n   211|  - **Rationale:** Concise WHY (1-2 sentences explaining reasoning)\n   212|  - **Reference:** Summarize evidence (e.g., \"Pattern verified across 5 components (86 instances)\") rather than listing all component:line numbers. Only include specific line numbers if critical for understanding.\n   213|  - **Implementation Strategy:** Plan HOW to implement (affected components from dependency graph, approach, verification steps). Planned during A1.1 when context is fresh, guides execution during A1.2. Must include chosen location with justification from architectural alternatives evaluation.\n   214|- Keep implementation details brief - only include when critical or non-obvious\n   215|- **Format:** Use prose descriptions, NOT code snippets, method signatures, or class definitions\n   216|- Avoid verbose bulleted lists - summarize key points instead\n   217|- Focus on WHAT and WHY, not detailed HOW (implementation details come during A1.2)\n   218|- **Grouping:** When multiple related [VERIFIED] decisions exist, consider grouping them under a single entry with a summary, then listing key decisions as sub-items for better readability\n   219|\n   220|**CRITICAL:** [PENDING] is acceptable when concrete design decisions require investigation of specific patterns. Investigation must be clearly specified (what patterns to investigate, what information needed).\n   221|\n   222|**TRANSPARENCY PRINCIPLE:**\n   223|\n   224|Documenting unknowns is valuable, not a failure:\n   225|- [PENDING] items show what still needs investigation (good transparency)\n   226|- ASSUMPTIONS section shows what needs verification (good transparency)\n   227|- [NOT READY] status shows what's blocking (good transparency)\n   228|- Multiple \"c\" cycles are expected - each cycle should resolve some unknowns\n   229|\n   230|The goal is not to eliminate all [PENDING] items in one cycle, but to:\n   231|1. Be transparent about what's known vs unknown\n   232|2. Progressively resolve unknowns through investigation\n   233|3. Reach [READY] when sufficient detail is available to begin implementation\n   234|\n   235|**FOR C3.3 (New components follow established patterns):**\n   236|- [ ] Checked tracker findings for existing patterns serving same role?\n   237|  - NO \u2192 Search for existing patterns before making design decision\n   238|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Checked tracker findings OR searched codebase, found pattern X at component Y:Z OR no existing pattern]\n   239|- [ ] If pattern exists, does design reuse it?\n   240|  - NO \u2192 Document why new pattern needed OR redesign to reuse\n   241|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Design reuses pattern X from component Y:Z]\n   242|- [ ] If pattern doesn't exist, verified no duplication?\n   243|  - NO \u2192 Search more thoroughly for similar patterns\n   244|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Searched for X pattern, found no existing implementations]\n   245|\n   246|**FOR C1.6 (Code duplication) and C2.1 (Error handling consistency):**\n   247|- [ ] Checked tracker findings for existing logic/patterns that could be reused?\n   248|  - NO \u2192 Review tracker findings OR search for existing implementations\n   249|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Checked tracker findings OR searched codebase]\n   250|- [ ] Design avoids duplicating existing logic?\n   251|  - NO \u2192 Extract common logic to reusable component OR reuse existing component\n   252|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Design reuses existing component X OR new logic is unique]\n   253|\n   254|**DESIGN PROPOSAL (Summary):**\n   255|\n   256|Design proposal shown when concrete information available, not just generic patterns.\n   257|\n   258|**BEFORE proposing design summary:**\n   259|\n   260|- [ ] Do you have concrete design decisions (not just design directions [DIRECTION])?\n   261|  - NO \u2192 Do NOT show generic design proposal. Show investigation plan for gathering information needed for concrete design.\n   262|  - YES \u2192 Continue to next check\n   263|\n   264|- [ ] Will I use prose descriptions (not code snippets or method signatures)?\n   265|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Design proposals must use prose, not code snippets or method signatures.\n   266|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Design will be described in prose]\n   267|\n   268|- [ ] ARCHITECTURAL ISSUES section reviewed?\n   269|  - Issues found? \u2192 Note impact, workaround exists?\n   270|  - No issues or workaround exists? \u2192 Continue\n   271|\n   272|- [ ] ASSUMPTIONS section checked?\n   273|  - Has entries? \u2192 Design summary must be CONDITIONAL and reference blockers\n   274|  - Empty? \u2192 Continue\n   275|\n   276|- [ ] DESIGN ISSUES section checked?\n   277|  - Has entries? \u2192 Design summary must be PARTIAL and reference blockers\n   278|  - Empty? \u2192 Continue\n   279|\n   280|**THEN:**\n   281|- If you only have design directions [DIRECTION] but no concrete decisions:\n   282|  - Do NOT show design proposal yet\n   283|  - Show investigation plan for gathering information needed for concrete design\n   284|  - Specify what patterns/behaviors/operations need investigation\n   285|  \n   286|- If you have concrete design decisions (even if [PENDING] or [CONDITIONAL]):\n   287|  - If ASSUMPTIONS or DESIGN ISSUES exist \u2192 Show CONDITIONAL or PARTIAL design\n   288|  - If ASSUMPTIONS and DESIGN ISSUES empty \u2192 Show COMPLETE design\n   289|  - Explicitly state: \"Design is conditional/partial - depends on resolving: [list ASSUMPTIONS and/or DESIGN ISSUES]\" (if applicable)\n   290|  - Reference: \"See ASSUMPTIONS section: [items]\" and/or \"See DESIGN ISSUES section: [items]\" (if applicable)\n   291|  - Summarize design decisions from DESIGN section (high-level design, patterns to follow, architectural decisions)\n   292|  - Keep summary concise - focus on key decisions and rationale, not exhaustive implementation details\n   293|  - **Format:** Use prose descriptions, NOT code snippets or method signatures\n   294|  - Implementation details as textual descriptions with minimal code (1-2 line code examples maximum, mainly prose)\n   295|  - DO NOT list method signatures, class definitions, or code structures - describe in prose instead\n   296|  - Reference specific design decisions from DESIGN section\n   297|  - Avoid verbose bulleted lists - summarize key points instead\n   298|\n   299|**WHEN NOT TO SHOW DESIGN PROPOSAL:**\n   300|\n   301|Do NOT show design proposal if:\n   302|- You only have design directions [DIRECTION] but no concrete design decisions\n   303|- Concrete design decisions would be mostly guesswork without investigation\n   304|- You haven't investigated patterns/behaviors needed for concrete design\n   305|\n   306|Instead:\n   307|- Show investigation plan for gathering information needed\n   308|- Make clear what needs investigation before concrete design can be made\n   309|- After investigation (\"c\" iteration), then show concrete design proposal\n   310|\n   311|**ALWAYS:**\n   312|- DO NOT write code files - user decides via \"i\"\n   313|- DO NOT include large code snippets or full class definitions\n   314|- DO NOT show method signatures, class definitions, or code structures in design proposals - use prose descriptions instead\n   315|- DO NOT retroactively label design decisions as [VERIFIED] after proposing\n   316|- DO NOT imply readiness to implement when ASSUMPTIONS or DESIGN ISSUES exist\n   317|- DO NOT generate markdown reports or documentation files\n   318|\n   319|**IMPLEMENTATION READINESS CHECKPOINT:**\n   320|\n   321|Before allowing \"i\" to proceed to A1.2, sufficient implementation details should be resolved through investigation cycles. Multiple \"c\" iterations are expected to progressively resolve unknowns.\n   322|\n   323|**IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS TRACKING:**\n   324|\n   325|During A1.1, as design decisions are made, document concrete implementation steps in the IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS section of the tracker:\n   326|\n   327|- **For each implementation step:**\n   328|  - File path(s) where changes will be made\n   329|  - Function/class names to create or modify\n   330|  - Parameter types and signatures (when critical for understanding)\n   331|  - Dependencies needed (services, imports, etc.)\n   332|  - Status: [RESOLVED] or [PENDING]\n   333|  \n   334|- **Mark as [RESOLVED] when:**\n   335|  - File path is known\n   336|  - Component structure is clear\n   337|  - Dependencies are identified\n   338|  - Pattern to follow is verified per V1 (read_file from reference components, not discovery alone)\n   339|  \n   340|**MANDATORY SELF-CHECK BEFORE MARKING [RESOLVED]:**\n   341|\n   342|\u26a0\ufe0f **CRITICAL:** Marking [RESOLVED] without completing this checkpoint is protocol violation.\n   343|\n   344|**BEFORE marking ANY [RESOLVED] status:**\n   345|\n   346|**FIRST:** Read COMPLETE reference implementation using read_file (not just structure, but full error handling, status updates, result structures, state changes)\n   347|\n   348|**THEN:** Complete ALL 8 lifecycle checklist items with code evidence:\n   349|\n   350|- [ ] Invocation pattern verified with code evidence?\n   351|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT mark [RESOLVED]. Add to ASSUMPTIONS or mark as [PENDING].\n   352|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [component:line references showing how component invoked, source data passed, where source originates]\n   353|\n   354|- [ ] Required source data verified with code evidence?\n   355|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT mark [RESOLVED]. Add to ASSUMPTIONS or mark as [PENDING].\n   356|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [component:line references showing all required source data, default values, data organization requirements]\n   357|\n   358|- [ ] Component access pattern verified with code evidence?\n   359|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT mark [RESOLVED]. Add to ASSUMPTIONS or mark as [PENDING].\n   360|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [component:line references showing how required components accessed (application state, passed as source data, created internally, etc.)]\n   361|\n   362|- [ ] Execution sequence verified with code evidence?\n   363|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT mark [RESOLVED]. Add to ASSUMPTIONS or mark as [PENDING].\n   364|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [component:line references showing all execution steps]\n   365|\n   366|- [ ] Success response pattern verified with code evidence?\n   367|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT mark [RESOLVED]. Add to ASSUMPTIONS or mark as [PENDING].\n   368|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [component:line references showing state changes, result data, return values on success]\n   369|\n   370|- [ ] Failure response pattern verified with code evidence?\n   371|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT mark [RESOLVED]. Add to ASSUMPTIONS or mark as [PENDING].\n   372|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [component:line references showing error handling mechanism, state changes, error messages on failure]\n   373|\n   374|- [ ] State changes verified with code evidence?\n   375|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT mark [RESOLVED]. Add to ASSUMPTIONS or mark as [PENDING].\n   376|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [component:line references showing persistent storage writes, external service calls, logging, other observable state changes]\n   377|\n   378|- [ ] Data organization requirements verified with code evidence?\n   379|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT mark [RESOLVED]. Add to ASSUMPTIONS or mark as [PENDING].\n   380|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [component:line references showing all required data organization, how it is created]\n   381|\n   382|**DECISION:**\n   383|- ALL 8 items verified with code evidence? \u2192 Mark [RESOLVED] with evidence list\n   384|- ANY item missing? \u2192 Mark as [PENDING] or add to ASSUMPTIONS, NOT [RESOLVED]\n   385|\n   386|**VIOLATION:** Marking [RESOLVED] without completing all 8 items is protocol violation.\n   387|  \n   388|- **Mark as [PENDING] when:**\n   389|  - Implementation detail cannot be determined without writing code\n   390|  - Must include: \"Why cannot resolve: [explanation]\" and \"Will resolve: [when/how]\"\n   391|\n   392|**PRE-FLIGHT CHECK BEFORE SHOWING [READY]:**\n   393|\n   394|**BEFORE changing Implementation Readiness to [READY]:**\n   395|\n   396|**FIRST:** For EACH [RESOLVED] item, explicitly list all 8 lifecycle checklist items with code evidence:\n   397|\n   398|**MANDATORY:** For each [RESOLVED] item in IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS, AI must list:\n   399|\n   400|1. Invocation: [component:line showing how invoked]\n   401|2. Required source data: [component:line showing all required data]\n   402|3. Component access: [component:line showing how components accessed]\n   403|4. Execution sequence: [component:line showing all execution steps]\n   404|5. Success response: [component:line showing return values/state changes on success]\n   405|6. Failure response: [component:line showing error handling/state changes on failure]\n   406|7. State changes: [component:line showing persistent writes/external calls]\n   407|8. Data organization: [component:line showing data structures/requirements]\n   408|\n   409|**ENFORCEMENT CHECK:**\n   410|\n   411|- [ ] For each [RESOLVED] item, listed all 8 lifecycle items with component:line evidence?\n   412|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT show [READY]. Complete missing lifecycle items for each [RESOLVED] item.\n   413|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [For each [RESOLVED] item: listed 8 lifecycle items with component:line references]\n   414|\n   415|- [ ] All 8 lifecycle items have component:line evidence (not generic descriptions)?\n   416|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT show [READY]. Replace generic descriptions with component:line evidence.\n   417|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [All lifecycle items reference specific component:line locations]\n   418|\n   419|- [ ] Success response pattern includes concrete return value/structure (not \"returns result\")?\n   420|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT show [READY]. Specify exact return value structure with component:line evidence.\n   421|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Success response shows exact structure from component:line]\n   422|\n   423|- [ ] Failure response pattern includes exact error handling mechanism (not \"handles errors\")?\n   424|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT show [READY]. Specify exact error handling with component:line evidence.\n   425|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Failure response shows exact mechanism from component:line]\n   426|\n   427|**THEN:** Check for hidden assumptions:\n   428|\n   429|- [ ] Any [RESOLVED] items that say \"probably\" or \"should work\"?\n   430|  - YES \u2192 CANNOT show [READY]. Move to [PENDING] or ASSUMPTIONS.\n   431|  - NO \u2192 Evidence: [No assumption language found]\n   432|\n   433|- [ ] Any [RESOLVED] items without component:line evidence for all 8 lifecycle items?\n   434|  - YES \u2192 CANNOT show [READY]. Move to [PENDING] or ASSUMPTIONS.\n   435|  - NO \u2192 Evidence: [All [RESOLVED] items have complete 8-item evidence]\n   436|\n   437|**DECISION:**\n   438|- ALL checks pass? \u2192 Show [READY]\n   439|- ANY check fails? \u2192 Show [NOT READY] with specific blocking items\n   440|\n   441|**READINESS CRITERIA:**\n   442|\n   443|**BEFORE allowing \"i\" command:**\n   444|\n   445|- [ ] Implementation Readiness = [READY]?\n   446|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Show blocking items from IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS section, suggest \"c\" to continue investigation.\n   447|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [All pre-flight checks passed]\n   448|\n   449|**Implementation Readiness Status:**\n   450|- [NOT READY]: \u2705 CORRECT STATE - Continue investigating with \"c\" (this is DEFAULT and EXPECTED state)\n   451|- [READY]: \u26a0\ufe0f EXCEPTION STATE - Only after completing ALL pre-flight checks\n   452|\n   453|**Default assumption:** Should be [NOT READY] after first investigation cycle. If [READY] after cycle 1, likely missed verification steps.\n   454|\n   455|**If NOT READY:**\n   456|- This is CORRECT and EXPECTED - not a failure\n   457|- Show what needs investigation (transparency is valuable)\n   458|- Suggest \"c\" to continue investigation (this is primary workflow)\n   459|- Each \"c\" cycle should resolve some unknowns and move closer to [READY]\n   460|- Do NOT allow \"i\" command until READY\n   461|\n   462|**If READY:**\n   463|- Show summary of implementation steps\n   464|- Note: Some minor [PENDING] items may remain - these can be resolved during implementation\n   465|- Allow \"i\" command to proceed to A1.2\n   466|\n   467|**IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS OUTPUT FORMAT:**\n   468|\n   469|Show in tracker as separate section:\n   470|\n   471|When marking [RESOLVED], MUST show evidence inline:\n   472|\n   473|```\n   474|IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS:\n   475|\u2705 Step 1: [Description] | [RESOLVED] | File: path/to/file.py, Function: function_name\n   476|   Evidence:\n   477|   - Invocation: component_a:42-45 (scheduler calls with args=[executor, config])\n   478|   - Component access: component_b:123 (gets service from request.app.state)\n   479|   - Execution: component_c:67-89 (creates job, calls function, updates status)\n   480|   - Success: component_c:91-95 (returns result dict)\n   481|   - Failure: component_c:97-102 (logs error, updates job status)\n   482|   - State changes: component_d:45 (writes to database)\n   483|   - Data org: component_e:23-30 (Job record structure)\n   484|\ud83d\udd0d Step 2: [Description] | [PENDING] | Why cannot resolve: [explanation] | Will resolve: [when/how]\n   485|```\n   486|\n   487|**If evidence is missing \u2192 Mark [PENDING] instead**\n   488|\n   489|**CRITICAL RULES:**\n   490|- Implementation details are CONCRETE (file paths, component names, dependencies), not abstract design\n   491|- [PENDING] items should have clear \"what needs investigation\" (not \"why blocked\")\n   492|- [PENDING] items naturally lead to [NOT READY] status - this is expected and normal\n   493|- Use \"c\" to continue investigation and resolve [PENDING] items in subsequent cycles\n   494|- [NOT READY] is DEFAULT state showing what still needs investigation\n   495|- Implementation details are separate from design decisions - design is WHAT/WHY, implementation details are HOW/WHERE\n   496|- Unverified patterns (discovered but not read via read_file) are assumptions \u2192 Add to ASSUMPTIONS section, not [RESOLVED]\n   497|- \"Pattern to follow is verified\" requires V1-style verification (read_file from reference components), not discovery alone\n   498|\n   499|**PROTOCOL VIOLATIONS - DO NOT:**\n   500|- \u274c Mark [RESOLVED] after only reading structure/pattern (must read complete implementation)\n   501|- \u274c Mark [READY] after first investigation cycle (default is 2-5+ cycles)\n   502|- \u274c Skip lifecycle checklist verification (all 8 items mandatory)\n   503|- \u274c Treat [NOT READY] as failure (it's default/correct state)\n   504|- \u274c Rush to [READY] to show progress (thoroughness > speed)\n   505|- \u274c Assume pattern understanding = complete verification (must verify with code evidence)\n   506|\n   507|**ITERATION (\"c\" - Widen Scope or Resolve Assumptions):**\n   508|\n   509|When user chooses \"c\" to continue investigation/design:\n   510|\n   511|**MANDATORY:** For each iteration:\n   512|- [ ] Identify NEW investigation targets:\n   513|  - Areas not yet investigated\n   514|  - Patterns needed for concrete design decisions (e.g., \"investigate all data access patterns to design abstraction layer methods\")\n   515|  - Deeper investigation of existing findings\n   516|  - Resolve ASSUMPTIONS from tracker\n   517|- [ ] For design-focused investigation: Investigate specific patterns/behaviors needed to make concrete design decisions\n   518|  - Specify what patterns/behaviors to investigate\n   519|  - Specify what information needed for concrete design\n   520|- [ ] For NEW targets: Use same verification rigor per V1\n   521|- [ ] Document NEW findings in tracker with proper status ([VERIFIED], [PARTIALLY VERIFIED], [PENDING])\n   522|- [ ] After investigation, make concrete design decisions based on findings\n   523|- [ ] Update DESIGN section with concrete decisions [VERIFIED] or mark as [PENDING] if more investigation needed\n   524|- [ ] **RE-EVALUATE PREVIOUS CONCLUSIONS:** When new evidence from NEW areas contradicts or clarifies previous findings:\n   525|  - Review existing FINDINGS and ARCHITECTURAL ISSUES in tracker\n   526|  - If new evidence shows a previous finding was incorrect or needs revision, update the finding with new evidence\n   527|  - Document the revision with reference to the new evidence that prompted the re-evaluation\n   528|  - This is NOT re-analyzing the same area - it's correcting conclusions based on new context from different areas\n   529|- [ ] **RE-EVALUATE READINESS STATE:** After updating findings, design decisions, or implementation details:\n   530|  - Review IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS section: Have any [RESOLVED] items become invalid due to new findings? Have any [PENDING] items been resolved?\n   531|  - Review IMPLEMENTATION READINESS status: Does current state of findings, design decisions, and implementation details still match the readiness status?\n   532|  - If implementation details changed (items resolved or invalidated), re-run PRE-FLIGHT CHECK (lines 382-429) to determine if readiness status should change\n   533|  - Update IMPLEMENTATION READINESS to [READY] or [NOT READY] based on current state of all dependencies\n   534|- [ ] DO NOT re-verify already [VERIFIED] findings without new evidence (reuse existing verified findings from tracker)\n   535|- [ ] DO NOT re-check already investigated checklist items in the same areas without new evidence\n   536|- [ ] Check for new violations introduced by new findings\n   537|- [ ] Update DESIGN ISSUES if new findings create problems discovered during investigation\n   538|- [ ] Update ASSUMPTIONS: If assumption verified, move to FINDINGS or DESIGN. If assumption incorrect, update design decisions.\n   539|- [ ] Update IN SCOPE and OUT OF SCOPE as investigation scope changes.\n   540|- [ ] Update tracker with NEW findings only (or revisions to existing findings when new evidence warrants)\n   541|\n   542|**BEFORE showing tracker to user (completeness checkpoint):**\n   543|- [ ] All violations found during this cycle documented in tracker?\n   544|- [ ] All relevant C1/C2/C3/P1 categories checked (not just the \"main\" issue)?\n   545|- [ ] Multiple violations from same pattern documented separately by category?\n   546|- [ ] No violations skipped because they seem \"secondary\" or will be \"fixed by larger changes\"?\n   547|- [ ] Tracker shows comprehensive view of ALL issues across ALL categories, not just prioritized subset?\n   548|- [ ] Each violation has proper category label (C1.4, C1.5, C1.6, C2.1, etc.)?\n   549|- [ ] Previous conclusions re-evaluated when new evidence from NEW areas contradicts them?\n   550|- [ ] Readiness state re-evaluated after updating findings/design/implementation details?\n   551|\n   552|**CRITICAL:** Iteration widens scope only. Reuse verified findings from tracker. Do NOT re-verify everything - only investigate NEW areas with full rigor. However, ALWAYS re-evaluate previous conclusions when new evidence from NEW areas contradicts or clarifies them. This ensures findings remain accurate as investigation scope expands.\n   553|\n   554|**ITERATION:** A1.1 continues until user chooses \"i\" to proceed to A1.2.\n   555|\n   556|**EXPECTED BEHAVIOR:**\n   557|- Multiple \"c\" cycles (2-5+) are NORMAL and EXPECTED\n   558|- Each \"c\" iteration should:\n   559|  - Resolve some [PENDING] items or ASSUMPTIONS\n   560|  - Add new findings or design decisions\n   561|  - Move closer to [READY] status\n   562|- Progress is incremental - don't expect to resolve everything in one cycle\n   563|- Transparency about unknowns ([PENDING], ASSUMPTIONS) is valuable throughout\n   564|\n   565|Each \"c\" iteration widens investigation scope to new areas, deepens investigation, or resolves assumptions from the ASSUMPTIONS section. Design decisions are made in each cycle as findings are discovered.\n   566|\n   567|### A1.2 IMPLEMENT\n   568|\n   569|**PROTOCOL ENTRY (AI SELF-CHECK):**\n   570|\n   571|- [ ] User chose \"i\" from menu?\n   572|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Show A1.1 tracker + menu + WAIT.\n   573|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [User message contains \"i\"]\n   574|\n   575|**BEFORE writing code:**\n   576|\n   577|- [ ] Implementation Readiness was [READY] when \"i\" command was issued?\n   578|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Return to A1.1 to resolve implementation details.\n   579|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Implementation Readiness was [READY] with all details resolved]\n   580|\n   581|- [ ] Design verified against architectural patterns (C1, C2, C3, P1)?\n   582|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Return to A1.1 DESIGN.\n   583|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Design verified]\n   584|\n   585|- [ ] Will I generate markdown reports or documentation files?\n   586|  - YES \u2192 CANNOT proceed. DO NOT generate markdown reports or documentation files.\n   587|  - NO \u2192 Evidence: [No markdown reports will be generated]\n   588|\n   589|- [ ] Implementation details from tracker available for reference?\n   590|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Implementation details should have been resolved during A1.1.\n   591|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Will reference IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS section from tracker]\n   592|\n   593|- [ ] Reviewed implementation strategy from DESIGN section?\n   594|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Review implementation strategy planned during A1.1 when context was fresh.\n   595|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Reviewed implementation strategy: approach, affected components, verification steps]\n   596|  - NOTE: Implementation strategy was planned during A1.1 when problem context was clear. Use it as starting point, but verify and adjust if needed.\n   597|\n   598|**DURING implementation:**\n   599|\n   600|**BEFORE writing each code section:**\n   601|- [ ] Checked tracker findings and design decisions for existing patterns/components to reuse?\n   602|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Review tracker findings and design tracker for existing patterns.\n   603|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Checked tracker findings and design decisions]\n   604|\n   605|FOR each code section being written:\n   606|- Verify against C1 (component boundaries, single responsibility, abstraction levels)?\n   607|  - **C1.6 (Code duplication):** Am I duplicating existing logic? If yes, reuse existing component.\n   608|- Verify against C2 (consistency, error handling, validation, atomic operations)?\n   609|  - **C2.1 (Error handling):** Am I using existing error handling pattern? If no, use existing pattern.\n   610|  - **C2.3 (Validation):** Am I duplicating validation logic? If yes, reuse existing validation functions.\n   611|- Verify against C3 (pattern and contract verification)?\n   612|  - **C3.3 (Follow established patterns):** Am I following existing patterns from tracker findings? If no, why not?\n   613|- Verify against P1 (expensive operations optimized, efficient data loading)?\n   614|- Violation found? Fix violation before continuing.\n   615|\n   616|**AFTER each implementation step:**\n   617|\n   618|- [ ] Code follows architectural patterns?\n   619|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Fix violations.\n   620|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Patterns followed]\n   621|\n   622|- [ ] No violations introduced?\n   623|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Fix violations.\n   624|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [No violations]\n   625|\n   626|- [ ] No duplication of existing patterns/logic (C1.6, C2.1, C3.3)?\n   627|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Refactor to reuse existing patterns/components.\n   628|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Reused existing pattern X from component Y:Z OR verified no existing pattern exists]\n   629|\n   630|### A1.3 VERIFY\n   631|\n   632|**SYSTEMATIC VERIFICATION:**\n   633|\n   634|Iterate through each checklist category (C1, C2, C3, P1) in order.\n   635|\n   636|FOR each checklist item:\n   637|- Search implementation for violations (grep/codebase_search)\n   638|- Read actual code per V1\n   639|- Decision point:\n   640|  - No violations found? \u2192 Mark [VERIFIED] with evidence\n   641|  - Violations found? \u2192 Mark [VIOLATION] with evidence, add to fix list\n   642|  - Not checked yet? \u2192 Optionally add to OUT OF SCOPE or ASSUMPTIONS as appropriate\n   643|\n   644|THEN: Show verification results to user.\n   645|\n   646|---\n   647|\n   648|## S1 - Status Indicators\n   649|\n   650|**Status meanings:**\n   651|\n   652|- **[DIRECTION]** = High-level design direction identified (does not require concrete details)\n   653|- **[PENDING]** = Not yet verified, needs investigation (for findings) OR needs investigation to make concrete (for design decisions) OR implementation detail cannot be resolved yet (must include justification)\n   654|- **[PARTIALLY VERIFIED]** = Found 1 component via read_file, needs more evidence (see V1 for verification requirements)\n   655|- **[VERIFIED]** = Verified with evidence per V1 (2-3+ components/contexts, code read from each, no violations, OR exception justified)\n   656|- **[CONDITIONAL]** = Depends on assumptions that need verification\n   657|- **[RESOLVED]** = Implementation detail is concrete and ready (file path, function name, dependencies all known)\n   658|- **[VIOLATION]** = Violation found in implementation (A1.3 only)\n   659|\n   660|---\n   661|\n   662|## TRACKER\n   663|\n   664|**Single structure tracks findings, design, assumptions, and scope:**\n   665|\n   666|```\n   667|FINDINGS:\n   668|\ud83d\udd0d Description | Category | [PENDING] | Next: search query\n   669|\ud83d\udd36 Description | Category | [PARTIALLY VERIFIED] | 1 component: component_name (needs 2-3+)\n   670|\u2705 Description | Category | [VERIFIED] | N components, X instances\n   671|   Pattern: concise explanation of the pattern/issue\n   672|\n   673|DESIGN:\n   674|\ud83c\udfaf Direction | Category | [DIRECTION] | High-level approach identified\n   675|\u2705 Decision | Category | [VERIFIED] | Decision summary | Rationale: concise explanation | Reference: [where]\n   676|   (For related decisions, group under summary with sub-items for key decisions)\n   677|\ud83d\udd36 Decision | Category | [CONDITIONAL] | Decision summary | Depends on: [assumption from ASSUMPTIONS section]\n   678|\ud83d\udd0d Decision | Category | [PENDING] | Decision summary | Needs: Investigation of [specific patterns/queries/operations]\n   679|\n   680|ARCHITECTURAL ISSUES:\n   681|\u274c Issue description | Category | Violates: [C1/C2/C3/P1 pattern]\n   682|   Impact: what this means for the codebase\n   683|   Found at: location description\n   684|\n   685|DESIGN ISSUES:\n   686|\ud83d\udea7 Issue description | Category | Blocks: [what] | Discovered during: [investigation/design]\n   687|   Context: where/why this issue was discovered\n   688|   Needs: [investigation/action]\n   689|\n   690|ASSUMPTIONS:\n   691|\u2753 Assumption description | Category | Needs verification: [what to check]\n   692|   Context: where/why this assumption was made\n   693|   Resolution: what investigation would verify or resolve this\n   694|(If no assumptions: Show empty section with note: \"*(No assumptions made during this investigation)*\")\n   695|\n   696|IN SCOPE:\n   697|\u2705 Area | Category | Currently investigating/designing\n   698|\n   699|OUT OF SCOPE:\n   700|\u23f8\ufe0f Area | Category | Not being addressed now\n   701|\n   702|IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS:\n   703|\u2705 Step 1: [Description] | [RESOLVED] | File: path/to/file.py, Function: function_name, Dependencies: [list]\n   704|   Verification: [List which of 8 lifecycle items verified with evidence]\n   705|\ud83d\udd0d Step 2: [Description] | [PENDING] | Why cannot resolve: [explanation] | Will resolve: [when/how]\n   706|   Missing lifecycle items: [List which of 8 items not yet verified]\n   707|\n   708|IMPLEMENTATION READINESS: [READY] / [NOT READY]\n   709|(If NOT READY: List blocking items and why)\n   710|```\n   711|\n   712|**Tracker rules:**\n   713|- Add findings immediately when noticed (start [PENDING] per S1)\n   714|- Mark [PARTIALLY VERIFIED] per S1 when: Found 1 component via read_file, but need V1 verification for [VERIFIED]\n   715|- **MANDATORY:** Before marking [VERIFIED] per S1, MUST complete VERIFICATION CHECKPOINT (see VERIFICATION REQUIREMENTS section below)\n   716|- Mark [VERIFIED] per S1 ONLY after: V1 verification complete + explicitly listed component/context names + no blocking issues + VERIFICATION CHECKPOINT passed\n   717|- Architectural violation found with 1 component (via read_file)? \u2192 Mark [PARTIALLY VERIFIED] per S1 in FINDINGS, do not add to ARCHITECTURAL ISSUES yet (need V1 verification, unless exception)\n   718|- Architectural violation verified per V1? \u2192 Mark [VERIFIED] per S1 in FINDINGS, add to ARCHITECTURAL ISSUES section with verified evidence\n   719|- Violation not verified yet (no read_file evidence)? \u2192 Keep [PENDING] per S1 in FINDINGS only, do not add to ARCHITECTURAL ISSUES until verified per V1\n   720|- Design issue discovered during investigation (problem affecting design approach)? \u2192 Add to DESIGN ISSUES section\n   721|- Add design decisions as they're made: Start with [DIRECTION] per S1 for high-level approach, then [PENDING] per S1 if needs investigation, [CONDITIONAL] per S1 if depends on assumptions, [VERIFIED] per S1 when complete\n   722|- Show tracker AFTER each cycle completes\n   723|- ARCHITECTURAL ISSUES: List violations verified per V1 (C1/C2/C3/P1 violations). Only add violations that are [VERIFIED] per S1. Violations with 1 component stay [PARTIALLY VERIFIED] per S1 in FINDINGS until V1 verification complete. Note impact but may not block design if workaround exists. Do not add violations based on grep/discovery alone - must verify per V1.\n   724|- DESIGN ISSUES: List problems discovered during investigation that affect design approach (not design decisions themselves). These are findings that impact design, similar to ARCHITECTURAL ISSUES but specifically for proposed design.\n   725|- ASSUMPTIONS: List assumptions made during investigation or design that need verification. These are things that are uncertain and need verification. Includes: patterns discovered but not verified via read_file, contracts assumed without reading reference components, implementation details marked as \"verified\" without V1-style verification. When using \"c\" to continue, prioritize resolving assumptions by investigating to verify or refute them. Once verified, move to FINDINGS or DESIGN as appropriate. If assumption is incorrect, update design decisions accordingly. **CRITICAL:** ASSUMPTIONS section must ALWAYS be shown in tracker output, even if empty (show with note: \"*(No assumptions made during this investigation)*\") for transparency.\n   726|- IN SCOPE: List areas currently being investigated or designed in this cycle.\n   727|- OUT OF SCOPE: List areas not being addressed in current investigation or implementation.\n   728|- IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS: List concrete implementation steps with file paths, function names, dependencies. Mark each as [RESOLVED] or [PENDING] with justification. **CRITICAL:** This section must be populated as design decisions are made during A1.1. Most items should be [RESOLVED] through investigation cycles before allowing \"i\" command, though some minor [PENDING] items are acceptable if they can be resolved during implementation.\n   729|- IMPLEMENTATION READINESS: Show [READY] or [NOT READY] status. If NOT READY, list what needs investigation (this is expected and normal). **CRITICAL:** Must be [READY] before allowing \"i\" command to proceed to A1.2. [NOT READY] is a normal, transparent state that progresses to [READY] through continued investigation cycles.\n   730|\n   731|---\n   732|\n   733|## V1 - Verification Standard\n   734|\n   735|**PRINCIPLE:** Verify systemic patterns, not isolated instances.\n   736|\n   737|**Verification count:**\n   738|- **Default:** Evidence from 2-3+ distinct components\n   739|- **Contextual:** Small codebase (<5 components) or monolithic structure \u2192 Evidence from 2+ distinct contexts/locations\n   740|- **Exception:** Single instance sufficient only if pattern unambiguous AND component is central/representative (document justification)\n   741|\n   742|**Tool usage:**\n   743|- **Discovery tools (grep/codebase_search):** Identify WHERE to look, cannot be used as evidence\n   744|- **Verification tool (read_file):** Must read actual code from identified locations to verify patterns\n   745|- **Rule:** grep/codebase_search identify locations \u2192 read_file verifies with actual code\n   746|\n   747|**Goal:** Distinguish patterns from outliers, mistakes, or isolated instances.\n   748|\n   749|---\n   750|\n   751|## VERIFICATION REQUIREMENTS\n   752|\n   753|**MANDATORY VERIFICATION CHECKPOINT - MUST COMPLETE BEFORE MARKING [VERIFIED]**\n   754|\n   755|**VERIFICATION PRINCIPLE:**\n   756|- See V1 - Verification Standard\n   757|\n   758|**STEP-BY-STEP WORKFLOW:**\n   759|\n   760|**STEP 1: Read code from multiple distinct contexts**\n   761|- Use read_file tool with specific offsets per V1\n   762|- Apply V1 verification count (default 2-3+ components, contextual 2+ contexts if monolithic/small)\n   763|- Example: `component_a:276; component_b:100` = 2 components \u2713\n   764|- Example (monolithic): `component_a:276 (method X); component_a:450 (method Y)` = 2 distinct contexts \u2713\n   765|- NOT: `component_a:276; component_a:299` = 1 component \u2717\n   766|\n   767|**STEP 2: List context names explicitly**\n   768|- Write out component/context names from read_file calls: [e.g., component_a, component_b OR component_a:method_X, component_a:method_Y]\n   769|- Count distinct components/contexts: [N]\n   770|- **CRITICAL:** Multiple offsets in same component = 1 component (unless distinct contexts in monolithic structure)\n   771|- **CRITICAL:** Must explicitly list names before proceeding\n   772|\n   773|**STEP 3: Count distinct components/contexts**\n   774|- Count: [N] components/contexts (NOT instance count, NOT line number count)\n   775|- Apply V1 verification count requirement\n   776|- Component/context count insufficient per V1 \u2192 STOP. Mark [PARTIALLY VERIFIED] per S1 (unless V1 exception applies - document justification).\n   777|- Component/context count sufficient per V1 \u2192 Continue to STEP 4\n   778|\n   779|**STEP 4: Check for violations FIRST**\n   780|- [ ] Checked for violations? (cross-layer dependencies violating architecture boundaries, expensive operations in loops)\n   781|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT mark [VERIFIED]. Check violations first.\n   782|  - YES \u2192 Continue\n   783|\n   784|**STEP 5: Verify code evidence**\n   785|- [ ] Read actual code per V1 using read_file tool (NOT grep results, NOT codebase_search snippets)?\n   786|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT mark [VERIFIED] per S1. Must call read_file tool per V1.\n   787|  - YES \u2192 Continue\n   788|\n   789|**STEP 6: Verify evidence completeness**\n   790|- [ ] Evidence includes: component:line references, component count (not instance count), pattern explanation, discovery path?\n   791|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT mark [VERIFIED]. Add missing evidence.\n   792|  - YES \u2192 Continue\n   793|\n   794|**STEP 7: Check blocking issues**\n   795|- [ ] No violations found OR violations verified and documented in ARCHITECTURAL ISSUES?\n   796|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT mark [VERIFIED]. Verify violations first.\n   797|  - YES \u2192 Continue\n   798|\n   799|**STEP 8: Decision**\n   800|- IF ALL STEPS PASS AND V1 verification complete \u2192 Mark [VERIFIED] per S1\n   801|- IF V1 exception applies (pattern unambiguous, component central/representative) \u2192 Mark [VERIFIED] per S1 with justification\n   802|- IF V1 verification incomplete \u2192 Mark [PARTIALLY VERIFIED] per S1\n   803|- OTHERWISE \u2192 Mark [PENDING] per S1\n   804|\n   805|---\n   806|\n   807|**When marking finding [VERIFIED], MUST collect internally (for AI verification) but show summary in output:**\n   808|\n   809|**INTERNAL VERIFICATION (AI must collect, not shown to human):**\n   810|1. **Component names** - List all component names from read_file calls: [e.g., component_a, component_b, component_c]\n   811|2. **Component count** - Count distinct components (NOT instance count): [N] components\n   812|3. **Instances** - Full component:line references per V1 (e.g., `component_a:42,58,63,77; component_b:32,45,99`)\n   813|   - **CRITICAL:** Apply V1 verification count (2-3+ different components, not just different offsets in same component)\n   814|   - **CRITICAL:** Must explicitly list component names internally (e.g., \"component_a, component_b\" = 2 components)\n   815|   - NOT: `component_a:276; component_a:299` = 1 component \u2192 [VERIFIED] per S1\n   816|   - CORRECT: `component_a:276; component_b:100` = 2 components \u2192 [VERIFIED] per S1\n   817|4. **Discovery path** - HOW discovered with tool call references: \"read_file calls #N, grep/codebase_search calls #M\"\n   818|   - Natural: \"Noticed while reading component:line_range\" OR targeted: \"Searched for pattern X\"\n   819|5. **Verification confirmation** - \"N different components confirmed via read_file\"\n   820|\n   821|**OUTPUT FORMAT (shown to human - concise summary only):**\n   822|- **Summary line**: \"Description | Category | [VERIFIED] | N components, X instances\"\n   823|- **Pattern line** (indented): \"Pattern: concise explanation of the pattern/issue\"\n   824|- DO NOT show detailed component lists, instance lists, or discovery paths in output\n   825|- Keep output concise to avoid cognitive overload for human\n   826|- Detailed evidence exists internally for AI verification but not displayed\n   827|\n   828|**NOT sufficient for [VERIFIED] per S1:**\n   829|- V1 verification incomplete (without exception justification) \u2192 Use [PARTIALLY VERIFIED] per S1 instead\n   830|- Found 2+ instances from same component (without distinct context separation per V1) \u2192 Use [PARTIALLY VERIFIED] per S1 instead\n   831|- Noticed pattern but didn't search systematically\n   832|- Based on filenames/comments without reading code\n   833|- Assumed pattern without verification\n   834|- **Grep counts or codebase_search snippets alone** (per V1: these are discovery tools, not verification)\n   835|- **\"Found N instances\" without reading actual code using read_file tool per V1**\n   836|- **Reading multiple offsets in same component** (counts as 1 component - need V1 verification)\n   837|- **Seeing code snippets in codebase_search results** (must still call read_file per V1)\n   838|- **Not explicitly listing component names before marking [VERIFIED]** \u2192 Must list component names and count them\n   839|- **Skipping VERIFICATION CHECKPOINT** \u2192 Must complete all 8 steps above\n   840|\n   841|**Sufficient for [PARTIALLY VERIFIED] per S1:**\n   842|- Found 1 component via read_file (even if multiple offsets from same component)\n   843|- Code evidence from read_file call per V1 (not grep/codebase_search snippets)\n   844|- Pattern explanation provided\n   845|- Component name: [e.g., component_a]\n   846|- Still needs V1 verification to reach [VERIFIED] per S1\n   847|\n   848|---\n   849|\n   850|## CATEGORY CHECKLISTS\n   851|\n   852|**Use to ensure breadth of investigation:**\n   853|\n   854|### C1 - Component Boundaries and Responsibilities\n   855|\n   856|- \u2611\ufe0f Component boundaries clear?\n   857|  \u2192 CHECK: Do components have single, well-defined responsibilities?\n   858|  \u2192 CHECK: Are boundaries between components explicit?\n   859|  \u2192 CHECK: Are unrelated concerns mixed in same component?\n   860|\n   861|- \u2611\ufe0f Dependency direction appropriate?\n   862|  \u2192 CHECK: Do dependencies flow in appropriate direction for your architecture?\n   863|  \u2192 CHECK: Are there circular dependencies?\n   864|  \u2192 CHECK: Do higher-level components depend on lower-level implementation details inappropriately?\n   865|\n   866|- \u2611\ufe0f Abstraction levels respected?\n   867|  \u2192 CHECK: Are implementation details hidden behind abstractions?\n   868|  \u2192 CHECK: Do components depend on interfaces/contracts rather than concrete implementations?\n   869|  \u2192 CHECK: Can implementation change without affecting consumers?\n   870|\n   871|- \u2611\ufe0f Concerns appropriately separated?\n   872|  \u2192 CHECK: Are core business logic concerns appropriately separated from infrastructure/interface concerns?\n   873|  \u2192 CHECK: Are core business logic components depending on infrastructure/interface types inappropriately?\n   874|  \u2192 CHECK: Are core business logic rules isolated from infrastructure/interface concerns?\n   875|  \u2192 NOTE: What \"concerns\" means depends on your architecture (layered, microservices, event-driven, functional, etc.)\n   876|\n   877|- \u2611\ufe0f Infrastructure concerns abstracted?\n   878|  \u2192 CHECK: Are infrastructure concerns (data access, external services, etc.) appropriately abstracted?\n   879|  \u2192 CHECK: Are there direct infrastructure calls scattered in core business logic?\n   880|  \u2192 CHECK: Are infrastructure operation boundaries clear?\n   881|  \u2192 NOTE: What \"infrastructure\" means depends on your architecture (database, APIs, message queues, file systems, etc.)\n   882|\n   883|- \u2611\ufe0f Code duplication across components?\n   884|  \u2192 CHECK: Is same logic repeated in multiple components?\n   885|  \u2192 CHECK: Are common patterns extracted to reusable components?\n   886|  \u2192 CHECK: Is core business logic duplicated unnecessarily?\n   887|\n   888|- \u2611\ufe0f Data structures appropriate?\n   889|  \u2192 CHECK: Do data structures contain only data, or do they also contain behavior?\n   890|  \u2192 CHECK: Is core business logic embedded in data structures where appropriate for your architecture?\n   891|\n   892|### C2 - Consistency and Patterns\n   893|\n   894|- \u2611\ufe0f Error handling uniform?\n   895|  \u2192 CHECK: Do components handle errors consistently?\n   896|  \u2192 CHECK: Are error propagation patterns uniform?\n   897|  \u2192 CHECK: Are failures explicit (not hidden)?\n   898|  \u2192 NOTE: Error handling approach depends on your architecture (exceptions, result types, event-driven failures, etc.)\n   899|\n   900|- \u2611\ufe0f Infrastructure access patterns consistent?\n   901|  \u2192 CHECK: Is access to infrastructure (data, external services, etc.) consistent?\n   902|  \u2192 CHECK: Are there ad-hoc access patterns scattered throughout?\n   903|  \u2192 CHECK: Are infrastructure operation boundaries clear?\n   904|  \u2192 NOTE: What \"infrastructure\" means depends on your architecture (database, APIs, message queues, file systems, etc.)\n   905|\n   906|- \u2611\ufe0f Validation rules centralized or scattered?\n   907|  \u2192 CHECK: Is validation logic duplicated across components?\n   908|  \u2192 CHECK: Are validation patterns consistent?\n   909|  \u2192 CHECK: How are constraints validated? (grep for validation patterns, verify per V1)\n   910|\n   911|- \u2611\ufe0f Consistency boundaries appropriately managed?\n   912|  \u2192 CHECK: Are consistency/atomicity boundaries properly bounded for your architecture?\n   913|  \u2192 CHECK: Are consistency boundaries managed consistently?\n   914|  \u2192 NOTE: Consistency model depends on your architecture (transactions, sagas, eventual consistency, etc.)\n   915|\n   916|- \u2611\ufe0f Naming and conventions consistent?\n   917|  \u2192 CHECK: Are naming conventions followed?\n   918|  \u2192 CHECK: Are similar operations named/handled similarly?\n   919|  \u2192 CHECK: Are patterns applied uniformly?\n   920|\n   921|### C3 - Pattern and Contract Verification\n   922|\n   923|- \u2611\ufe0f Contracts verified before use?\n   924|  \u2192 CHECK: Are contracts/interfaces verified to exist before using them?\n   925|  \u2192 CHECK: Are component boundaries verified (what's exposed, how accessed)?\n   926|  \u2192 CHECK: Are actual identifiers checked (naming varies by codebase)?\n   927|\n   928|- \u2611\ufe0f Patterns extracted from existing code?\n   929|  \u2192 CHECK: Are all existing components serving same role identified?\n   930|  \u2192 CHECK: Is the pattern structure extracted (how are they structured)?\n   931|  \u2192 CHECK: Are 2-3+ examples verified before applying pattern broadly?\n   932|\n   933|- \u2611\ufe0f New components follow established patterns?\n   934|  \u2192 CHECK: Do new components follow existing patterns when similar components exist?\n   935|  \u2192 CHECK: Are reference implementations studied before creating new components?\n   936|  \u2192 CHECK: Is pattern consistency verified per V1?\n   937|\n   938|- \u2611\ufe0f Contract modifications handled correctly?\n   939|  \u2192 CHECK: Is current contract verified before modification?\n   940|  \u2192 CHECK: Are all call sites identified before contract changes?\n   941|  \u2192 CHECK: Are contract changes implemented with all call sites together?\n   942|\n   943|### P1 - Performance and Efficiency\n   944|\n   945|- \u2611\ufe0f Expensive operations optimized?\n   946|  \u2192 CHECK: Are expensive operations repeated unnecessarily?\n   947|  \u2192 CHECK: Are operations that access external resources/data repeated in loops?\n   948|  \u2192 CHECK: Could multiple accesses be batched together?\n   949|\n   950|- \u2611\ufe0f Data/resource loading efficient?\n   951|  \u2192 CHECK: Are related data/resource items loaded together when needed?\n   952|  \u2192 CHECK: Is loading strategy appropriate for your architecture? (eager/lazy/batch as needed)\n   953|  \u2192 CHECK: Is data/resource loading causing repeated access when it could be optimized?\n   954|  \u2192 NOTE: Loading strategy depends on your architecture (ORM lazy loading, graph DB traversal, API batching, etc.)\n   955|\n   956|- \u2611\ufe0f Large collections handled efficiently?\n   957|  \u2192 CHECK: Are large collections loaded without pagination/streaming?\n   958|  \u2192 CHECK: Are collections processed in batches when possible?\n   959|  \u2192 CHECK: Is memory usage appropriate for data volume?\n   960|\n   961|- \u2611\ufe0f Computations done at appropriate level?\n   962|  \u2192 CHECK: Are aggregations/computations done at the appropriate level for your architecture?\n   963|  \u2192 CHECK: Are computations repeated unnecessarily?\n   964|  \u2192 CHECK: Is work delegated to the appropriate layer for your architecture?\n   965|  \u2192 NOTE: Appropriate level depends on your architecture (database, service, client, etc.)\n   966|\n   967|---\n   968|\n   969|## OUTPUT\n   970|\n   971|**TRACKER DISPLAY RULES:**\n   972|- **During A1.1 INVESTIGATE + DESIGN:** Show unified tracker (findings + design + issues + implementation details + readiness) after EACH investigation cycle (with menu)\n   973|- **During A1.2 IMPLEMENT:** DO NOT show tracker (implementation phase)\n   974|- **During A1.3 VERIFY:** Show tracker with verification results\n   975|\n   976|**AFTER each investigation cycle, show:**\n   977|\n   978|1. **Current Scope Indicator** - Show: \"INVESTIGATION CYCLE N: [scope description]\" (e.g., \"INVESTIGATION CYCLE 1: Initial architecture review\", \"INVESTIGATION CYCLE 2: Data access patterns\")\n   979|2. **Tracker** - Unified tracker showing FINDINGS + DESIGN + ARCHITECTURAL ISSUES + DESIGN ISSUES + ASSUMPTIONS + IN SCOPE + OUT OF SCOPE + IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS + IMPLEMENTATION READINESS\n   980|   - Format tracker with clear section headers and visual separation\n   981|   - Use icons/emojis to highlight key findings (\u2705 verified, \u26a0\ufe0f issues, \ud83d\udd0d to investigate)\n   982|   - **FINDINGS entries:** Summary line with component/instance counts, then concise pattern explanation on indented line (detailed evidence collected internally by AI but not shown to avoid cognitive overload)\n   983|   - **ARCHITECTURAL ISSUES:** Format with Impact and Found at on separate indented lines for readability\n   984|   - **ASSUMPTIONS:** MUST always be shown, even if empty (show with note: \"*(No assumptions made during this investigation)*\") for transparency\n   985|   - **IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS:** Show concrete implementation steps with file paths, function names, dependencies. Mark each as [RESOLVED] or [PENDING] with justification.\n   986|   - **IMPLEMENTATION READINESS:** Show [READY] or [NOT READY] status. If NOT READY, list blocking items clearly.\n   987|   - Design section populated as decisions are made in each cycle\n   988|3. **Evidence gathered** - Brief summary of what was verified this cycle (1-2 sentences, not duplication of tracker)\n   989|   - Use \ud83d\udd0d icon for discovery, \u2705 for verification\n   990|4. **Next proposal** - Plan/scope for next investigation cycle OR design refinement areas\n   991|   - What to investigate next (new areas, deeper investigation)\n   992|   - OR design refinement areas if design decisions need more investigation\n   993|   - Present as PLAN, not OPTIONS\n   994|   - ARCHITECTURAL ISSUES exist? \u2192 Note impact, workaround needed? (use \u26a0\ufe0f icon)\n   995|   - DESIGN ISSUES exist? \u2192 MUST propose investigating those areas (use \u26a0\ufe0f icon)\n   996|   - IMPLEMENTATION READINESS = [NOT READY]? \u2192 List what needs investigation (this is expected and normal) and suggest \"c\" to continue (use \u26a0\ufe0f icon)\n   997|   - Design summary: High-level design + implementation details (prose descriptions only, NO code snippets or method signatures, 1-2 line code examples maximum if critical) - shown when investigation scope sufficient\n   998|   - Use \ud83d\udccb icon for design proposals\n   999|   - ONLY USER decides via \"i\" when to begin coding (only available when Implementation Readiness = [READY])\n  1000|5. **Menu** - Format clearly with visual separation\n  1001|   - **Show menu:** After EACH investigation cycle (always available during A1.1), during A1.2, during A1.3\n  1002|   - **Context-driven:** Show ONLY options for current phase (A1.1 shows c/i, A1.2 shows c/v, A1.3 shows c/d)\n  1003|   - **Title:** \"**Menu:**\" (no phase prefix)\n  1004|   - **Note:** \"i\" option should be disabled/not shown if Implementation Readiness = [NOT READY] (show message explaining why)\n  1005|\n  1006|**OUTPUT RULES:**\n  1007|- Tracker = single source of truth - AI collects detailed evidence internally for verification, but shows concise summary to human\n  1008|- Show unified tracker after EACH investigation cycle (with menu)\n  1009|- FINDINGS entries: Summary line (component/instance counts) + concise pattern explanation (detailed evidence collected internally by AI but not displayed)\n  1010|- ARCHITECTURAL ISSUES: Use formatted layout with Impact and Found at on separate indented lines\n  1011|- **DESIGN entries:** Group related decisions when possible for better readability. Summarize references (e.g., \"across 5 components (86 instances)\") rather than listing all component:line numbers unless critical. Use sub-items for key decisions when grouping.\n  1012|- \"Evidence gathered\" = brief summary (1-2 sentences) of new verifications this cycle\n  1013|- \"Next proposal\" references ASSUMPTIONS from tracker (for verification) OR design refinement areas, does not duplicate design\n  1014|- DO NOT show checklist iteration or duplicate tracker content\n  1015|- **Flow:** Investigation cycles continue until user chooses \"i\" to implement - menu always available\n  1016|- **CRITICAL:** AI must still complete all verification steps internally per V1 (document discovery paths, etc.) even though detailed evidence is not shown in output\n  1017|\n  1018|**Menu enforcement (BEFORE showing menu):**\n  1019|- [ ] Am I showing ONLY options for current phase?\n  1020|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Show only options for current phase (A1.1: c/i, A1.2: c/v, A1.3: c/d).\n  1021|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Menu shows only current phase options]\n  1022|- [ ] Menu title is \"**Menu:**\" (no phase prefix)?\n  1023|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Use \"**Menu:**\" as title, not \"During... Menu:\".\n  1024|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Title format correct]\n  1025|\n  1026|---\n  1027|\n  1028|## MENU\n  1029|\n  1030|**Menu Display (show after each cycle):**\n  1031|\n  1032|Format menu with clear visual separation and icons. Show ONLY options for current phase:\n  1033|\n  1034|**During A1.1 INVESTIGATE + DESIGN:**\n  1035|```\n  1036|---\n  1037|\n  1038|**Menu:**\n  1039|- \ud83d\udd0d **c** - continue: More investigation/design iteration\n  1040|- \u2705 **k** - check: Targeted C*/P* checklist verification (e.g., \"k C3\" or \"k all\")\n  1041|- \ud83d\ude80 **i** - implement: Start implementation\n  1042|\n  1043|... or anything else?\n  1044|\n  1045|---\n  1046|```\n  1047|\n  1048|**During A1.2 IMPLEMENT:**\n  1049|```\n  1050|---\n  1051|\n  1052|**Menu:**\n  1053|- \u27a1\ufe0f **c** - continue: Continue implementation\n  1054|- \u2705 **v** - verify: Check implementation against architectural patterns\n  1055|\n  1056|... or anything else?\n  1057|\n  1058|---\n  1059|```\n  1060|\n  1061|**During A1.3 VERIFY:**\n  1062|```\n  1063|---\n  1064|\n  1065|**Menu:**\n  1066|- \u27a1\ufe0f **c** - continue: More verification\n  1067|- \u2705 **k** - check: Targeted C*/P* checklist verification (e.g., \"k C3\" or \"k all\")\n  1068|- \u2728 **d** - done: Feature complete\n  1069|\n  1070|... or anything else?\n  1071|\n  1072|---\n  1073|```\n  1074|\n  1075|**Menu Formatting Rules:**\n  1076|- Show menu after EACH investigation cycle (always available during A1.1), during A1.2, during A1.3\n  1077|- Show ONLY options for current phase (context-driven)\n  1078|- Use horizontal rule (`---`) before and after menu for visual separation\n  1079|- Use consistent icons for each menu option\n  1080|- Bold the command letter for quick scanning\n  1081|- Title: \"**Menu:**\" (no phase prefix in title)\n  1082|\n  1083|**Menu Options:**\n  1084|\n  1085|**c - continue:**\n  1086|- Available: After EACH investigation cycle (during A1.1), during A1.2, during A1.3\n  1087|- A1.1: More investigation/design iteration (widens scope: new areas, deeper investigation, NOT readiness to implement)\n  1088|- A1.2: Continue writing code\n  1089|- A1.3: Continue verification checks\n  1090|\n  1091|**BEFORE responding to \"c\" during A1.1:**\n  1092|- [ ] Will I propose specific NEW investigation targets (areas not yet investigated)?\n  1093|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Propose specific NEW areas to investigate.\n  1094|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Specific NEW areas listed]\n  1095|- [ ] Will I re-evaluate previous conclusions if new evidence contradicts them?\n  1096|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Must re-evaluate previous findings when new evidence from NEW areas contradicts them.\n  1097|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Will review existing FINDINGS and ARCHITECTURAL ISSUES, update if new evidence contradicts]\n  1098|- [ ] Will I re-evaluate readiness state after updating findings/design/implementation details?\n  1099|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Must re-evaluate IMPLEMENTATION READINESS when dependencies change.\n  1100|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [Will review IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS and re-run PRE-FLIGHT CHECK if needed, update IMPLEMENTATION READINESS status]\n  1101|- [ ] Will I re-verify already [VERIFIED] findings without new evidence?\n  1102|  - YES \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Reuse verified findings from tracker, only investigate NEW areas unless new evidence warrants re-evaluation.\n  1103|  - NO \u2192 Evidence: [Will only investigate NEW areas, reuse verified findings unless new evidence contradicts them]\n  1104|- [ ] Will I imply implementation readiness?\n  1105|  - YES \u2192 CANNOT proceed. \"c\" means continue investigating, not implement.\n  1106|  - NO \u2192 Evidence: [Response proposes investigation, not implementation]\n  1107|\n  1108|**i - implement:**\n  1109|- Available: During A1.1\n  1110|- Invokes: A1.2 IMPLEMENT phase\n  1111|\n  1112|**BEFORE responding to \"i\" command:**\n  1113|- [ ] Mandatory verification checkpoint passed (lines 330-345)?\n  1114|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. All [RESOLVED] items must pass verification checkpoint before [READY]. Continue with \"c\" to verify.\n  1115|  - YES \u2192 Evidence: [All [RESOLVED] items verified per checkpoint requirements]\n  1116|  \n  1117|- [ ] Implementation Readiness = [READY]?\n  1118|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Show blocking items from IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS section, suggest \"c\" to continue investigation.\n  1119|  - YES \u2192 Continue to next check\n  1120|  \n  1121|- [ ] All implementation details are [RESOLVED] OR explicitly deferred with justification?\n  1122|  - NO \u2192 CANNOT proceed. Show [PENDING] items that need resolution, suggest \"c\" to continue investigation.\n  1123|  - YES \u2192 Continue to next check\n  1124|  \n  1125|- 