s) is typically handled at the AI model level or by Cursor's cloud infrastructure, not in the proxy logs.\\n\\nHowever, your concern about context growing beyond what can be handled is valid. The proxy.log might show:\\n1. The actual requests being sent to the backend\\n2. Response times\\n3. Error messages when requests fail\\n4. General interaction patterns\\n\\nBut it wouldn't show the specific token count or compression behavior that happens internally.\\n\\nIf you want to monitor token usage m [... omitted end of long line]\n889:          \"text\": \"<workspace_result workspace_path=\\\"/home/g/dev/local/runpod\\\">\\nFound 50 matching lines\\nlogs/proxy.log\\n1:[INFO] Loaded Qwen tokenizer for accurate token counting\\n4:[DEBUG] Proxy config: backend=http://localhost:8000, context_limit=131072, compression=off\\n14:      \\\"content\\\": \\\"You are an AI coding assistant, powered by Qwen3-Coder 30B Q4_K_XL (match benchmark).\\\\n\\\\nYou operate in Cursor.\\\\n\\\\nYou are a coding agent in the Cursor IDE that helps the USER with software engineering tasks.\\\\n\\\\nEach time the USER sends a message, we may automatically attach information about their current state, such as what files they have open, where their cursor is, recently viewed files, edit history in their session so far, linter errors, and more. This information is provided in case it is helpful to the task.\\\\n\\\\nYour main goal is to follow the USER's instructions, which are denoted by the <user_query> tag.\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n<system-communication>\\\\n- Tool results and user messages ma [... omitted end of long line]\n920:          \"text\": \"Looking at the proxy.log more carefully, I can see the token usage information in the debug messages. From what I can see in the logs:\\n\\n```\\n[DEBUG] ---- context (request_id: req-47a4051be8ce) ----\\n[DEBUG]   messages_in: 12, tokens_in: 49102\\n[DEBUG]   tool_condense: 0 response(s) condensed, tokens_after_condense: 49102\\n[DEBUG]   messages_out: 12,